  "summary": "San Francisco announced it will keep its Flock ALPR cameras but impose new access restrictions, requiring leadership approval and a tie to an active investigation for data queries, shortening Flock retention to 30 days (SFPD keeps a copy up to a year), and mandating regular audits by the Department of Police Accountability. DA Brooke Jenkins is also working with Assemblymember Matt Haney on state legislation to criminalize misuse of ALPR data.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Under the new policy, officers will need approval from leadership and a connection to an active criminal investigation to search data tied to the city's network of automated license plate readers (ALPR).",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "The data will also be removed from Flock's platform after 30 days. San Francisco police will keep their own copy for up to a year.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "District Attorney Brooke Jenkins said she asked state Assemblymember Matt Haney to work on state legislation that would make it a crime for a police officer or anyone else to access ALPR data for personal or inappropriate reasons.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    }
  ],
